usage: mathc attest-batch [--manifest=PATH] [--from=decisions] [--out=DIR] [--kind=...] --producer=ID [--run=CMD] [--result=pass|fail] [--dry-run]
  --manifest  <path> read rows from TSV manifest (overrides --from)
  --from  <kind> source of obligations: decisions (default) | none
  --out  <dir> output directory (default $MATH_CODING_ATTESTATIONS_DIR or .math-coding/attestations)
  --kind  <s> kind_ for --from=decisions (default review)
  --producer  <id> producer identity for --from=decisions (REQUIRED; there is no default)
  --result  <pass|fail> result for --from=decisions (default pass)
  --run  <cmd> command to run once; its exit code becomes --result and its output digest becomes materials_digest. Required with --kind=test|build|analysis|observation.
  --dry-run  print JSON to stdout, no files
  --skip-failing  do not write an attestation for an obligation whose own verifier FAILED. The obligation is reported on stderr and left unsigned: an existing signature is not overwritten with a failure, and no signature is invented for a check that did not pass.
  --only-changed  re-sign only the obligations whose decision SCOPE intersects the files changed since BASE. Under semantics A a scope change invalidates the attestation that covered it, so this is what a commit that touches a scope must run to keep the gate honest.
  --run-per-obligation  execute EACH row's own verifier and use its verdict as the result. The verifier strings come from the decision file - the same ones `mathc verify` executes. Without this flag a runtime kind has to be signed by one --run for the whole batch, which is one claim about many obligations.
  -help  Display this list of options
  --help  Display this list of options
